Hello,

I don't know about everyone else, but I think the Dragons lack in flavour,
partially from uninteresting names. But even the Unique dragons are kind
of weird. I suppose the different breath weapons provide some variation,
but is it really enough? Then in the Dragon Armor thread, someone
suggested finishing the implementation of the Shimmering Dragon, which
would be another dangerous but bland dragon.

So, here are some ideas (with little or no expectation of them ever being
picked up officially):
Chromatic Dragon: Should be called Tiamat, like in the dictionary and in
Babylonian folklore.
Ixoth: From the legend of Merlin, the Red Dragon represents the
Welsh/British people and a White Dragon represents the invading Saxons.
The Knight nemesis should therefore be a white dragon.
 Where does the name Ixoth come from? The red dragon of Wales is
(possibly) called Dewi.
 I suppose Ixoth is red so that he can breathe flame, but perhaps he can
be a fire-breathing dragon that happens to be white. And have a
Welsh or Irish inspired name.

Perhaps either of the unique dragons could produce (eventually) unique
dragon scale mail. Not better, maybe even with drawbacks (Tiamat armour
could offend/aggravate every dragon), but would make good souvenirs. You
couldn't wish for them, but could only get them in the traditional killing
way. (You might have to wish up Tiamat, though).

For the other dragons, maybe some substitutions of legendary names for D&D
names (this may require some changes in ability/physiology).
Firedrake: Instead of Red

Lindworm: Instead of White (primarily from Nordic Legends) (no
wings/flying), no claw attacks, make it
faster instead (Marco Polo described them as fast runners)

Lung/Long Dragon: Instead of Orange (from East Asian Legends)
 (no wings, can fly (no coding change))

Leviathan:(no wings, can't fly, can swim/ hide underwater)
Instead of Blue (or Green, Black...)

Wyvern: no claw attacks, add a sting attack,
Instead of Green (Poison seems to fit the wyvern legend)

Guivre: instead of Yellow (or whatever), no limbs, can't fly. (add
hugging?)

That's all I've come up with, so far.

Opinions? Criticisms? Other ideas?

-Nick Webb

